<Guide lang="en" slug="finca-apartment-or-village-house-bed-and-breakfast" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="aankoopkeuring" ctaTitle="Got your eye on a property for your B&B?" ctaText="We check whether the rooms, bathrooms, water and drainage are fit for guests, and what the conversion will cost.">
  <h2 id="core">Legally, a B&amp;B does not exist</h2>
  <p>In Andalusia, “bed and breakfast” is not an official category. What you want to do falls under one of the forms of tourist letting or accommodation recognised by the Junta de Andalucía, each with its own requirements. Which form is possible depends mainly on <strong>where the house is</strong> (residential or rural land), <strong>its legal status</strong> and <strong>whether you live there yourself</strong>. That is why choosing between a finca, village house and apartment matters far more for a B&amp;B than for simply living there. For the general trade-offs, see the <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/">main guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="forms">The forms that do exist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Letting rooms in your own home</strong>: as a <em>vivienda con fines turísticos</em> (VFT) by the room. You live there and let a few rooms to tourists. Only possible on residential land, so in a village or on the coast.</li>
    <li><strong>Letting a whole house or guest annexe</strong>: as a VFT for the whole home. Again only on residential land. Since 2024 town halls may limit the number of holiday lets.</li>
    <li><strong>Rural accommodation</strong> (<em>alojamiento rural</em>), such as a <em>casa rural</em>: for houses on rural land or in small villages, with their own requirements for the condition of the house, furnishing and surroundings.</li>
    <li><strong>A small guesthouse (pensión or hostal)</strong>: falls under the hotel rules, with stricter requirements on fire safety, accessibility and a business activity licence from the town hall.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each form is entered in the Andalusian tourism register (<em>Registro de Turismo de Andalucía</em>). You use that number to advertise on letting platforms. The national registration number required since July 2025 was struck down by Spain’s Supreme Court in May 2026. The rules change regularly; have the options for a specific property checked with the town hall and a specialist gestor or lawyer before you sign the preliminary contract.</p>

  <h2 id="finca">A B&amp;B in a finca</h2>
  <p>This is the classic dream: a restored cortijo with a pool, a few guest rooms and breakfast on the terrace with a sea view. Guests looking for peace and nature will gladly pay for it. But there are three points where it often goes wrong:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The land</strong>: a finca sits on rural land, so the standard VFT scheme does not apply. You work under the rural tourism rules, which set requirements for the house and its surroundings.</li>
    <li><strong>The legal status</strong>: many fincas have AFO status. Then you may not extend: no extra rooms, no guest annexe in the old stable. Whether an AFO property may be let to tourists varies case by case. Read our guide to <a href="/en/guides/afo-legalisation-andalusia/">AFO and legalisation</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The services</strong>: six or ten guests use far more water than two residents. Can the water supply cope, even in a dry summer? Can the septic tank handle it? And can guests find and drive the access track after dark?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a B&amp;B, then, a finca is both the most beautiful and the most difficult property. Choose one with fully permitted rooms, a reliable water supply and a good road. See also <a href="/en/guides/buying-a-finca-in-spain/">buying a finca</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="village">A B&amp;B in a village house</h2>
  <p>Legally, a village house is often simpler: it sits on residential land, so letting as a VFT (by the room if you live there, or as a whole home) is possible in principle, unless the town hall limits holiday lets. Popular villages such as Frigiliana, Cómpeta and Nerja attract tourists all year round, and guests can walk to restaurants and sights.</p>
  <p>The downsides: the house is narrow and tall, so rooms each with their own bathroom do not always fit. Parking for guests is often difficult in the old centre. And the walls are shared with neighbours, who do not always appreciate the sound of suitcases and late arrivals. In protected village centres there are also rules on façades and roofs when you renovate.</p>

  <h2 id="apartment">And an apartment?</h2>
  <p>An apartment is the least suitable for a B&amp;B. Letting rooms with breakfast in an apartment is small-scale and barely profitable, and since April 2025 a new tourist let in an apartment building needs prior approval from three-fifths of the community of owners. If you want to let an apartment, you are looking at a holiday let rather than a B&amp;B. See the page on a <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/holiday-home/">holiday home</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="works">What the conversion involves</h2>
  <p>Guests expect more than a resident. For a B&amp;B, budget for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An en-suite bathroom per room: €6,000 – €25,000 each.</li>
    <li>Air conditioning and heating in the rooms, which is also a requirement for tourist letting in Andalusia: €3,000 – €10,000 for several rooms.</li>
    <li>Sound insulation between rooms, good lighting and locks.</li>
    <li>Fire safety: smoke alarms, extinguishers and a clear escape route; stricter rules for hotel-type forms.</li>
    <li>On a finca, often larger water storage, a new treatment plant (€3,000 – €10,000) and improvements to the access track.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For prices per item, see our guide to <a href="/en/guides/renovation-costs-spain/">renovation costs</a>. We are happy to quote for the <a href="/en/renovation/">renovation</a> of your B&amp;B, broken down per room.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">Checklist before you buy</h2>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Land classification known: residential or rural</li>
    <li>Legal status of the house, including all rooms, extensions and pool</li>
    <li>Checked with the town hall whether tourist letting is (still) possible</li>
    <li>Chosen form of letting or accommodation, and its requirements</li>
    <li>Water, drainage and power sized for the number of guests</li>
    <li>Access track and parking for guests</li>
    <li>Conversion budget per room, including bathrooms and air conditioning</li>
    <li>Tax consequences discussed with a gestor (income tax, VAT, possibly self-employed registration)</li>
    <li>A <a href="/en/property-survey/">property survey</a> with a price per defect</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not living there all year, or want help with changeovers and supervision? Have a look at <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>.</p>
  <p class="note">This page gives general information and is not legal or tax advice. Tourist-letting rules change regularly.</p>
</Guide>
